[al:新概念英语第四册]
[ti:How to Grow Old]
[00:00.56]Lesson 11 | 第11课
[00:02.03]How to grow old | 如何安度晚年
[00:04.95]What, according to the author, is the best way to overcome the fear of death as you get older? | 照作者看来，随着年事增高，克服对死亡的恐惧的最佳方法是什么？
[00:19.36]Some old people are oppressed by the fear of death. | 有些老年人害怕死亡。
[00:24.72]In the young there is a justification for this feeling. | 这种情绪如果发生在年轻人身上，是情有可原的。
[00:28.64]Young men who have reason to fear that they will be killed in battle | 青年人有理由担心他们会战死沙场，
[00:33.06]may justifiably feel bitter in the thought | 想到他们没有享受到人生最美好的东西，
[00:36.68]that they have been cheated of the best things that life has to offer. | 因而感到痛苦，这也是情有可原的。
[00:41.96]But in an old man who has known human joys and sorrows, | 但是，一个已经尝过人间甘苦的老人，
[00:47.07]and has achieved whatever work it was in him to do, | 并且已经做完了他力所能及的一切工作，
[00:51.59]the fear of death is somewhat abject and ignoble. | 如果他仍然害怕死亡，那就有几分可怜又可鄙了。
[00:56.54]The best way to overcome it--so at least it seems to me-- | 克服这种恐惧的最好办法——至少在我看来是如此——
[01:02.14]is to make your interests gradually wider and more impersonal, | 是使你的兴趣逐渐扩大，变得更加超然物外，
[01:07.79]until bit by bit the walls of the ego recede, | 直到自我的界限一点点地消退，
[01:12.90]and your life becomes increasingly merged in the universal life. | 你的生命越来越融入到普遍的生命之中。
[01:18.32]An individual human existence should be like a river-- | 人的个体生命应该像一条河流——
[01:23.16]small at first, narrowly contained within its banks, | 最初很小，被狭窄的河岸限制着，
[01:28.31]and rushing passionately past boulders and over waterfalls. | 汹涌地冲过巨石，飞跃瀑布。
[01:33.23]Gradually the river grows wider, the banks recede, the waters flow more quietly, | 渐渐地，河流变得宽阔，河岸后退，水流也变得更加平静，
[01:40.88]and in the end, without any visiblebreak, they become merged in the sea, | 最终，在没有任何明显的断裂的情况下，它们汇入了大海，
[01:47.18]and painlessly lose their individual being. | 并且毫无痛苦地失去了它们的个体存在。
[01:50.92]The man who, in old age can see his life in this way, | 那些年老时能以这种方式看待自己生命的人，
[01:56.52]will not suffer from the fear of death, | 将不会受到对死亡的恐惧的折磨，
[01:59.30]since the things he cares for will continue. | 因为他所珍视的一切将会继续存在。
[02:03.00]And if, with the decay of vitality, weariness increases, | 而且，如果随着活力的衰退，疲劳感增加，
[02:10.06]the thought of rest will be not unwelcome. | 休息的念头将不会令人不快。
[02:13.94]I should wish to die while still at work, | 我希望在工作时死去，
[02:17.46]knowing that others will carry on what I can no longer do, | 知道别人会继续我无法再做的事情，
[02:22.09]and content in the thought that what was possible has been done. | 并满意地想到，所有可能做的事情都已经完成了。